{
  "title": "pytest for xUnit people",
  "summary": "Everything you know from xUnit, in pytest: test functions and plain assert, parametrize for theories, pytest.raises, ids that name each case, and how a red pytest run reads.",
  "minutes": 14,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "The same ideas, less ceremony",
      "body": [
        "pytest finds files named `test_*.py` and runs every function named `test_*`. There is no `Assert` class: a plain `assert` is rewritten by pytest to show both sides when it fails.",
        "",
        "| xUnit v3 | pytest |",
        "|---|---|",
        "| `[Fact] public void Fifty_ships_free()` | `def test_fifty_ships_free():` |",
        "| `Assert.Equal(0m, Shipping.Cost(50m))` | `assert cost(50) == 0` |",
        "| `[Theory]` + `[InlineData(...)]` | `@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"total, expected\", [(49.99, 4.99), (50, 0)])` |",
        "| `Assert.Throws<ArgumentOutOfRangeException>(...)` | `with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"negative\"):` |",
        "| `IClassFixture<T>`, constructor setup | `@pytest.fixture` (scope `function`, `module`, `session`) |",
        "| `ITestOutputHelper` | `capsys`, `caplog` fixtures, or `print` (shown on failure) |",
        "| `Assert.Equal(0.3, x, precision)` | `assert x == pytest.approx(0.3)` |",
        "| `--filter-method \"*fifty*\"` | `pytest -k fifty` |",
        "",
        "```python fragment",
        "import pytest",
        "from decimal import Decimal",
        "",
        "def cost(total: Decimal) -> Decimal:",
        "    return Decimal(\"0\") if total >= 50 else Decimal(\"4.99\")",
        "",
        "@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"total, expected\", [",
        "    (Decimal(\"49.99\"), Decimal(\"4.99\")),",
        "    (Decimal(\"50\"), Decimal(\"0\")),",
        "], ids=[\"just under\", \"boundary\"])",
        "def test_shipping(total, expected):",
        "    assert cost(total) == expected",
        "```",
        "",
        "Money in Python is `Decimal` (from strings), for the same reason it is `decimal` in C#: `0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3` in binary floating point."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "match",
      "prompt": "Match the xUnit idea to its pytest spelling.",
      "pairs": [
        ["`[Theory]` with `[InlineData]` rows", "`@pytest.mark.parametrize`"],
        ["`Assert.Throws<T>`", "`with pytest.raises(T):`"],
        ["a class fixture shared by a class", "`@pytest.fixture(scope=\"module\")` or `\"class\"`"],
        ["`Assert.Equal(expected, actual, precision: 9)`", "`assert actual == pytest.approx(expected)`"],
        ["run only matching tests", "`pytest -k fifty`"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "A parametrized test fails. Which line does pytest print to identify the case?",
      "code": [
        "@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"score, letter\", [(90, \"A\"), (89, \"B\")])",
        "def test_grade(score, letter):",
        "    assert grade(score) == letter"
      ],
      "options": [
        "`FAILED test_grade.py::test_grade[89-B]`",
        "`FAILED test_grade.py::test_grade`",
        "`FAILED test_grade.py line 3`",
        "`FAILED parametrize case 2`"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Each case gets an id built from its parameters (or from `ids=`), like xUnit's `Score_maps_to_a_letter(score: 89, ...)`. `pytest \"test_grade.py::test_grade[89-B]\"` reruns exactly that case."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "title": "Grade boundaries with parametrize",
      "prompt": [
        "`grades.py` is the Python port of the grading service:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "def grade(score: int) -> str:",
        "    if score < 0 or score > 100:",
        "        raise ValueError(f\"score must be 0 to 100, got {score}\")",
        "    if score >= 90:",
        "        return \"A\"",
        "    if score >= 80:",
        "        return \"B\"",
        "    if score >= 70:",
        "        return \"C\"",
        "    if score >= 60:",
        "        return \"D\"",
        "    return \"F\"",
        "```",
        "",
        "Write the tests (`from grades import grade`): one **parametrized** test with both sides of every boundary, and one parametrized test of the refused scores with `pytest.raises(ValueError)`. At least **10** cases in all. **Run checks** runs them with pytest on the real module, then on six copies with a bug planted in each."
      ],
      "starter": [
        "import pytest",
        "from grades import grade",
        "",
        "",
        "def test_ninety_five_is_an_a():",
        "    assert grade(95) == \"A\""
      ],
      "solution": [
        "import pytest",
        "from grades import grade",
        "",
        "",
        "@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"score, letter\", [",
        "    (100, \"A\"), (90, \"A\"), (89, \"B\"), (80, \"B\"), (79, \"C\"),",
        "    (70, \"C\"), (69, \"D\"), (60, \"D\"), (59, \"F\"), (0, \"F\"),",
        "])",
        "def test_letter_for_score(score, letter):",
        "    assert grade(score) == letter",
        "",
        "",
        "@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"score\", [-1, 101])",
        "def test_scores_outside_0_to_100_are_refused(score):",
        "    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"0 to 100\"):",
        "        grade(score)"
      ],
      "pytest": {
        "module": "grades.py",
        "minTests": 10,
        "target": [
          "def grade(score: int) -> str:",
          "    if score < 0 or score > 100:",
          "        raise ValueError(f\"score must be 0 to 100, got {score}\")",
          "    if score >= 90:",
          "        return \"A\"",
          "    if score >= 80:",
          "        return \"B\"",
          "    if score >= 70:",
          "        return \"C\"",
          "    if score >= 60:",
          "        return \"D\"",
          "    return \"F\""
        ],
        "mutants": [
          { "name": "an A needs 91", "replace": [["score >= 90", "score > 90"]] },
          { "name": "a B needs 81", "replace": [["score >= 80", "score >= 81"]] },
          { "name": "a C needs 71", "replace": [["score >= 70", "score > 70"]] },
          { "name": "a D needs only 59", "replace": [["score >= 60", "score >= 59"]] },
          { "name": "101 is accepted", "replace": [["score > 100", "score > 101"]] },
          { "name": "negative scores are accepted", "replace": [["score < 0 or ", ""]] }
        ]
      },
      "require": [
        { "pattern": "@pytest\\.mark\\.parametrize", "message": "Uses @pytest.mark.parametrize" },
        { "pattern": "pytest\\.raises\\(", "message": "Checks the refused scores with pytest.raises" }
      ],
      "hints": [
        "A list of `(score, letter)` tuples is the parametrize table: `(90, \"A\"), (89, \"B\"), ...`.",
        "Every `>=` needs the value on it and the one below it.",
        "Refused scores: `@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"score\", [-1, 101])` and `with pytest.raises(ValueError): grade(score)`."
      ],
      "explain": "Same table, same six bugs as the C# version: the craft is identical, only the spelling changes. pytest's case ids (`[89-B]`) do what xUnit's display names do."
    }
  ]
}
